    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im a freelance designer and im trying to get my books ready for the end of the year... i just bought this expensive ergonomic chair for my desk and im not sure if i should list it as a business expense or if its just a personal draw? how does the irs usually look at stuff like office furniture?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The IRS treats office furniture like an ergonomic chair as a business expense, but you'll need to consider how long you plan to use it. If it's a relatively inexpensive item, you can usually deduct the full cost in the year you buy it. For a more expensive piece, you might need to depreciate it over several years, which is a bit more complex, but it's definitely a business asset, not a personal draw. It's always wise to keep receipts and documentation to support your claim.",
